From 3b87da200afe88f7553a5bcacf40ddaaf7782975 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 09:39:01 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 13/18] hwmon: macsmc: Register fans as thermal cooling devices

On the M4 Pro MacBook Pro (J616s) the SMC keeps both fans in its own
mode 3. With no operating system asking it for cooling, it leaves them
off, or switches them on late and ramps them slowly, while the dies
heat up: four busy CPU cores took the CPU die from 52 C to 95 C in two
minutes with both fans at 0 rpm. Linux has to ask for the cooling.

With #cooling-cells = <3> on the hwmon node, every fan registers a
cooling device, named by its index among the fan-* nodes. State 0
leaves the fan to the SMC; states 1 to 8 run it at speeds spread evenly
from its minimum to its maximum, through the same manual mode and Ftst
unlock as the fan_control target, which they do not need. A fan runs at
the higher of the userspace target and its cooling state, so userspace
cannot take it below what the thermal zones ask for, and it goes back
to the SMC once neither asks for anything.

Unlocking takes the SMC seconds, so states are applied from a work item.
Any failure leaves the fan with the SMC. Suspend, shutdown and removal
stop the work and hand every fan back; resume applies the cooling
states again. Without #cooling-cells nothing changes.

KUnit covers the state to speed mapping, cooling without fan_control,
cooling together with a userspace target, the stopped state and a
failed unlock.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
---
 drivers/hwmon/macsmc-hwmon-test.c | 104 +++++++++++-
 drivers/hwmon/macsmc-hwmon.c      | 264 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++----
 2 files changed, 332 insertions(+), 36 deletions(-)

